Bellows — C++20 Concept-Oriented Machine-Learning Library
=========================================================

Bellows is an independent GPLv3 C++ machine-learning library. It
follows the GNU Coding Standards as a style convention; it is not part
of the GNU Project and is not affiliated with the Free Software
Foundation.

Overview
--------

Bellows provides a non-derivative, concept-oriented C++20 architecture
for statistical learning, tabular preprocessing, unified second-order
stagewise gradient boosting, composable pipelines, model evaluation, and
low-latency binary inference.

Rather than porting dynamic Python mixin hierarchies or string-keyed C
handles, Bellows models machine-learning capabilities as structural
C++20 concepts (`bellows::estimator`, `bellows::supervised_fittable`,
`bellows::unsupervised_fittable`, `bellows::predictor`,
`bellows::belief_predictor`, `bellows::transformer`,
`bellows::categorical_aware`, `bellows::missing_aware`,
`bellows::tunable`, and `bellows::serialisable`) and free customization
point objects (`bellows::fit`, `bellows::predict`,
`bellows::predict_beliefs`, `bellows::transform`, `bellows::infer_one`).

Key Capabilities
----------------

  * Schema-aware tabular domain (`bellows::data_table` and
    `bellows::table_view`) with first-class real, nominal, and ordinal
    column scales and native missing-value representation.
  * Unified stagewise gradient boosting (`bellows::stagewise_booster`)
    parameterised over orthogonal policies:
      - Second-order differentiable losses: squared residual, pseudo-Huber,
        logistic Bernoulli, and multinomial deviance.
      - Split cut policies: exact sorted midpoints (`exact_cut_policy`) and
        histogram quantile buckets (`quantile_bin_policy`) with sibling
        histogram subtraction.
      - Tree topology growth policies: level-synchronous
        (`depth_synchronous_growth`), best-gain priority frontier
        (`best_gain_frontier_growth`), and balanced symmetric decision
        tables (`oblivious_symmetric_growth`).
      - Observation sampling policies: uniform sampling
        (`uniform_full_sample`) and gradient-magnitude filtering
        (`gradient_magnitude_filter`).
      - First-class missing-value split routing and leakage-free ordered
        target statistics (`with_ordered_categories`).
  * Preprocessing and feature engineering: `moment_normaliser`,
    `interval_rescaler`, `column_mender`, `indicator_expander`,
    `ordered_target_encoder`, `quantile_bucketer`, `dispersion_filter`,
    and `relevance_selector`.
  * Classical estimators and ensembles: `penalised_linear_regressor`,
    `penalised_logistic_classifier`, `proximity_learner`,
    `gaussian_density_classifier`, `centroid_partitioner`, and
    `subspace_tree_ensemble`.
  * Composition and model selection: compile-time pipe chains (`|`),
    runtime `dynamic_chain`, `fold_splitter`, `stratified_splitter`,
    `cross_validate`, `parameter_sweep`, and composable evaluation metric
    function objects.
  * Versioned binary serialisation (`bellows::save_artifact`,
    `bellows::load_artifact`) and zero-allocation single-row inference
    (`bellows::infer_one`).

Building, Testing, and Installing
---------------------------------

Bellows requires a C++20 compiler (such as `g++` 12 or later) and Make
(or CMake 3.20+).

To build the static and shared libraries (`libbellows.a`, `libbellows.so`),
the CLI utilities (`bellows`, `bellows-train`, `bellows-infer`), and the
test driver (`bellows-check`):

    make all

To run the complete unit, integration, CLI, and GNU style verification
suite:

    make check

To install headers, libraries, `bellows.pc`, and binaries under `PREFIX`
(default `/usr/local`):

    make install PREFIX=/usr/local

To remove all generated build artefacts:

    make clean

To create a source distribution archive (`bellows-0.1.0.tar.gz`):

    make dist

Command-Line Utilities
----------------------

All executables support `--help` and `--version`:

  * `bellows`       — Inspect datasets, model artefacts, and run benchmarks.
  * `bellows-train` — Fit a Bellows model from a CSV/tabular dataset and
                      serialise a binary `.blws` artefact.
  * `bellows-infer` — Load a binary `.blws` model artefact and generate
                      predictions on new tabular observations.
  * `bellows-check` — Execute the Bellows automated verification suite.

